				<h1>Vibe Kanban alternative: a local Board for coding agents</h1>
				<div class="post-meta">By Minh Ngo · Published <time datetime="2026-11-21">21 November 2026</time> · Updated <time datetime="2026-11-21">21 November 2026</time></div>
				<p class="lede">You may not need one: Vibe Kanban is open source, community maintained and still released, and its local workspaces keep working after the company behind it shut down. If you want a native desktop app instead, Anywhere is one option: free, MIT licensed, no account, with a Board where each started ticket gets its own git worktree and agent session. It lacks some things Vibe Kanban has, such as a web UI, inline diff comments and a preview browser.</p>
				<div class="callout">
					<p><span class="callout-title">Disclosure</span>The Anywhere team wrote this. Vibe Kanban facts come from its <a href="https://github.com/BloopAI/vibe-kanban">GitHub README</a>, <a href="https://github.com/BloopAI/vibe-kanban/releases">releases</a>, <a href="https://vibekanban.com/docs/getting-started">docs</a> and <a href="https://vibekanban.com/blog/shutdown">shutdown post</a>, checked on 21 November 2026. If something here is out of date, <a href="https://github.com/mingo023/anywhere/issues">open an issue</a>.</p>
				</div>

				<h2 id="what-happened">What happened to Vibe Kanban?</h2>
				<p>On 10 April 2026, Louis Knight-Webb wrote in <a href="https://vibekanban.com/blog/shutdown">Goodbye bloop</a>: "Today we're shutting down bloop, the company behind Vibe Kanban. The Vibe Kanban project will live on, open source and community maintained." The post says the vast majority of users were free users and the team couldn't find a business model it could get excited about.</p>
				<p>The same post set out what changed. Remote services stayed up for 30 days and were then removed, after which Vibe Kanban moves to "a fully local architecture". The services named are "kanban issues, comments, projects and organisations". The latest version at the time added a data export. And: "For the avoidance of doubt, local workspaces will continue to function." The <a href="https://github.com/BloopAI/vibe-kanban">README</a> now opens with "Vibe Kanban is sunsetting." and a link to that post.</p>

				<h2 id="do-you-need-to-switch">Do you need to switch?</h2>
				<p>Not necessarily. The project is still shipping. <a href="https://github.com/BloopAI/vibe-kanban/releases/tag/v0.1.46.1794744340">v0.1.46</a> was published on GitHub on 15 November 2026, with community contributors, and includes work such as installing and running agents from the ACP registry and turning off usage analytics and crash reports. The repository is Apache-2.0. If your local workspaces still do what you need, keep using them.</p>
				<p>The case for looking elsewhere is narrower: you relied on the hosted parts the shutdown post lists, such as kanban issues and organisations, or you would rather run a desktop app than a local server in a browser tab. Vibe Kanban's own <a href="https://vibekanban.com/docs/getting-started">Get Started page</a> says that without signing in, "the kanban board, issues, and team features will be unavailable", so if the board is the part you use, check how it behaves in the release you run.</p>

				<h2 id="what-a-replacement-needs">What should a replacement have?</h2>
				<p>Vibe Kanban's model is plan, run, review. A replacement worth the move covers the same three steps:</p>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>A board.</strong> Tickets you write ahead of time, with the agent and the branch chosen per ticket, and columns that show which ones are running and which need you.</li>
					<li><strong>Isolation.</strong> Each piece of work in its own git worktree and branch, so agents don't edit the same checkout. Vibe Kanban <a href="https://vibekanban.com/docs/workspaces/creating-workspaces">creates a git worktree</a> per workspace; a replacement should do no less.</li>
					<li><strong>Review.</strong> The diff next to the session that made it, and a path to a pull request and a merge.</li>
				</ul>

				<h2 id="anywhere-board">How does Anywhere's Board work?</h2>
				<p>Anywhere is a desktop app for running many coding agents at once. A background service, pocketd, owns the terminals and sessions, and it runs the Board. The <a href="/docs/board">Board</a> holds tickets for every project in one place.</p>

				<h3>Write a ticket</h3>
				<p>Click <strong>Board</strong> in the sidebar, then the composer, or press <kbd>N</kbd>. The first line you type becomes the title and the whole text becomes the prompt. Chips set the project, the agent, the base branch, and optionally a priority and a label. Only git repositories can hold tickets.</p>

				<h3>Start it</h3>
				<p>Click <strong>Start</strong> on the card, press <kbd>⌘↩</kbd> in the composer to add and start at once, or drag the card to Working. A started ticket goes to Queued first. When a slot is free, pocketd creates the ticket's worktree from its base branch and starts its session there. Board sessions start with the agent's own permission settings.</p>

				<h3>Limit how many run at once</h3>
				<p><strong>Work on at once</strong>, in the Board's Display menu, caps how many tickets are starting, working or waiting on you. It is 4 by default and goes from 1 to 8. The Working column's header shows how many slots are in use.</p>

				<h3>Follow the columns</h3>
				<p>Once a ticket has a session, its card follows the session's status through eight columns: <strong>To do</strong>, <strong>Queued</strong>, <strong>Working</strong>, <strong>Needs you</strong> (waiting for an answer, failed or stopped), <strong>Done</strong> (turn ended, not seen yet), <strong>In review</strong> (idle), <strong>Merged</strong> and <strong>Closed</strong>. A merged pull request closes the ticket and puts it in Merged. Closing a ticket by hand keeps its worktree and session, and <strong>Restore</strong> brings it back.</p>
				<p>Review happens beside the session: <a href="/docs/review">Changes</a> shows the diff, can compare the worktree with its base branch as a pull request would, and commits, pushes and opens the pull request. The pull request card shows checks, reviews and merge state, and its <strong>Send</strong> buttons hand a failed check or a GitHub review comment to the agent.</p>

				<h2 id="cli">Can you add tickets from the command line?</h2>
				<p>Yes. The <a href="/docs/cli"><code>anywhere</code> command</a> adds, reads, edits and deletes Board tickets:</p>
				<pre><code>anywhere ticket create --project app --provider codex --base main --start "Fix the flaky login test"
anywhere ticket list
anywhere ticket read &lt;id&gt;
anywhere ticket update --prompt "Also cover the logout path" &lt;id&gt;
anywhere ticket delete &lt;id&gt;</code></pre>
				<p><code>--start</code> starts the ticket at once; it waits in Queued until a slot is free. Pass <code>-</code> as the prompt to read it from standard input. The command has no flags for priority or label; set those on the Board. On the Mac, install the command from <strong>Settings › General</strong>. For scripting sessions as well as tickets, see <a href="/blog/script-claude-code-codex-from-cli">Script coding agents from your shell</a>.</p>

				<h2 id="side-by-side">How do Vibe Kanban and Anywhere compare?</h2>
				<div class="table-wrap">
				<table>
