				<h2 id="when-vibe-kanban-may-fit-better">When may Vibe Kanban fit better?</h2>
				<div class="callout">
					<p><span class="callout-title">Vibe Kanban is the better pick if you want</span></p>
					<ul>
						<li>One command, <code>npx vibe-kanban</code>, and a UI in the browser, with no app to install.</li>
						<li><a href="https://vibekanban.com/docs/reviewing-code">Inline comments on diff lines</a> sent back to the agent.</li>
						<li>A <a href="https://vibekanban.com/docs/browser-testing">built-in preview browser</a> with devtools and device modes.</li>
						<li>Workspaces that span <a href="https://vibekanban.com/docs/workspaces">several repositories</a>.</li>
						<li>Agents Anywhere doesn't run, such as Gemini CLI, GitHub Copilot, Amp, Droid or Qwen Code (<a href="https://vibekanban.com/docs/supported-coding-agents">supported agents</a>).</li>
						<li><a href="https://vibekanban.com/docs/remote-access">Remote access</a> from another device, or a <a href="https://vibekanban.com/docs/self-hosting/deploy-docker">self-hosted</a> instance.</li>
						<li>To keep a setup that already works. Its local workspaces still run.</li>
					</ul>
				</div>

				<h2 id="moving-over">How do you move a workflow over?</h2>
				<ol>
					<li>Install and sign in to your agent CLIs, for example <code>claude auth login</code> or <code>codex login</code>. The full list is in <a href="/docs/agents">Supported agents</a>.</li>
					<li>Install Anywhere and add each repository as a project.</li>
					<li>Move setup scripts, such as <code>npm install</code>, into <code>.anywhere/config.json</code> or <code>.anywhere/setup.sh</code>, so new <a href="/docs/worktrees">worktrees</a> are ready before the agent starts.</li>
					<li>Recreate open issues as tickets. There is no importer, so add them on the Board or with <code>anywhere ticket create</code>.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>For the wider field, see <a href="/blog/best-tools-run-multiple-coding-agents">the best tools to run multiple coding agents in parallel</a> and <a href="/blog/conductor-alternative">Anywhere vs Conductor</a>.</p>

				<section class="faq" aria-labelledby="faq">
					<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
					<h3>What is a good Vibe Kanban alternative?</h3>
					<p>First check whether you need one: Vibe Kanban is open source, community maintained and still released, and its local workspaces keep working. If you want a native desktop app instead, Anywhere is a free, MIT-licensed option with a Board where each started ticket gets its own git worktree and agent session. It has no web UI, inline diff comments or preview browser.</p>
					<h3>Is Vibe Kanban still maintained?</h3>
					<p>Yes, by its community. bloop, the company behind it, shut down on 10 April 2026 and said the project will live on, open source and community maintained. Remote services such as kanban issues, comments, projects and organisations were removed after 30 days, and local workspaces keep working. Version 0.1.46 was published on GitHub on 15 November 2026.</p>
					<h3>Does Anywhere have a kanban board for coding agents?</h3>
					<p>Yes. The Board holds tickets for every project. A ticket has a title, a prompt, an agent, a project and a base branch, and optionally a priority and a label. A started ticket waits in Queued until a slot is free, then gets its own worktree and session. It runs up to 4 tickets at once by default, adjustable from 1 to 8.</p>
					<h3>Which agents does Anywhere support?</h3>
					<p>Ten agent CLIs that you install and sign in to yourself: Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Grok Build, OpenCode, Pi, omp, fx, Hermes Agent and Antigravity. A ticket can use any of them.</p>
					<h3>Is Anywhere free?</h3>
					<p>Yes. Anywhere is MIT licensed, with no account, no cloud tier and no premium tier. It runs the agent CLIs you already use.</p>
				</section>
